*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    dir = !profile_dir(username)
*
*    The profile directory Windows would give an account of this name, IF
*    something is already sitting there.  Empty when the path is clear, which
*    is the ordinary case.
*
*      status 0   answered - the return value is the path, or empty
*      status 2   the name is not a valid OS user name
*      status 1   PowerShell could not be run, so nothing was measured
*
*    ***AN EMPTY RETURN WITH A NON-ZERO STATUS MEANS "NOT MEASURED", NOT "THE
*    PATH IS CLEAR".***  The two are indistinguishable in the return value and
*    the caller must read STATUS() before acting on the empty, or it will
*    report a clear path on a run that never looked.
*
*    WHY THIS EXISTS.  A directory left at C:\Users\<name> makes Windows put
*    the NEXT account of that name at C:\Users\<name>.<COMPUTERNAME> instead -
*    silently.  Anything under the old home, ssh authorized_keys included, is
*    then not where the account looks for it, and the symptom that reaches the
*    user is "public-key authentication is refused", days later and nowhere
*    near the cause.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 36: the ruling is that
*    CREATE.ACCOUNT refuses and names the directory, rather than creating an
*    account that is wrong in a way nothing says out loud.
*
*    IT CANNOT CLEAR THE PATH ITSELF AND DOES NOT TRY.  The usual reason a
*    directory survives its account is that Windows still has the profile's
*    registry hive mounted, which locks a file inside it - and nothing short of
*    a restart releases that.  So this reports, and DELETE_USER's reclaim
*    record plus the boot sweep (gplbld/reclaim-profiles.ps1) are what
*    eventually remove it.
*
*    THE ROOT IS READ, NOT ASSUMED.  ProfilesDirectory under the ProfileList
*    key is where Windows keeps it, it is a REG_EXPAND_SZ, and it is not always
*    C:\Users - a machine that has moved its profiles elsewhere would otherwise
*    have every one of these checks answer about a directory nobody uses.
*    C:\Users is the fallback for a machine that has no value at all.
*
*    NO ELEVATION.  Reading the key and testing a path need none, so this is a
*    plain OS.EXECUTE ... CAPTURING rather than !ps_script - which also means
*    it works in an ordinary session, before CREATE.ACCOUNT has asked anybody
*    for anything.  OS_GROUP's LISTMEM makes the same call for the same reason:
*    !ps_script returns an exit code and this needs the script's OUTPUT.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function profile_dir(username)
$catalog !profile_dir

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

   dir = ''
   set.status 0

* The name is interpolated into the command below, so it is validated first -
* !valid_os_name permits letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen only, so
* it cannot carry a quote out of the string.
   if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
      set.status 2
      return dir
   end

   ps = '$root = ""; '
   ps := '$pl = "HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\ProfileList"; '
   ps := 'try { $root = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $pl -Name ProfilesDirectory '
   ps := '-ErrorAction Stop).ProfilesDirectory } catch { }; '
   ps := 'if ($root -eq "") { $root = Join-Path $env:SystemDrive "Users" }; '
* REG_EXPAND_SZ, so %SystemDrive%\Users arrives unexpanded.  Joined and tested
* as written it would answer false for a directory that is really there.
   ps := '$root = [Environment]::ExpandEnvironmentVariables($root); '
   ps := '$d = Join-Path $root "' : username : '"; '
   ps := 'if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $d) { Write-Output $d }; '
   ps := 'exit 0'

   os.execute ps capturing out

   rc = os.error()
   if rc # 0 then
      set.status 1
      return dir
   end

* THE CARRIAGE RETURN HAS TO COME OFF and TRIM does not take it - it removes
* spaces.  PowerShell ends every line CRLF and the capture splits on the LF,
* so the path arrives with a CR still on it; OS_GROUP's LISTMEM carries the
* same conversion and the measurement behind it.  A path with a CR on the end
* would be printed by the caller as a blank line after the message.
   lines = dcount(out, @fm)
   for ln = 1 to lines
      nm = out<ln>
      convert char(13) : char(10) to '' in nm
      nm = trim(nm)
      if nm # '' and dir = '' then dir = nm
   next ln

return dir
end

* END-CODE
